  "query_preview": "..\nToday through Friday...\n\nThe current weather system exits this morning, ending the current\nchance for precipitation and allowing for mostly clear skies into\nearly Sunday. Breezy winds will linger longest over the east but \nare expected to decrease by early this afternoon. Highs will be \nmuch cooler this weekend, in the 40s to low 50s.\n\nLate Sunday into Monday, an upper low clips southeast Montana, \nallowing for a chance (20-50%) of light showers over the \nsoutheast. As temperatures decrease overnight, rain will change\nover to snow, although little to no snow accumulation is expected.\nCurrently, there is a less than 20% chance for more than a 0.1\" of\nsnow outside of the higher hills.\n\nLate Monday into Wednesday, a more impactful system is expected to\nbring a better chance (30-50% over the plains, 60-80% over the\nmountains) for widespread precipitation. Highs will remain in the\n40s to low 50s. While the exact timing and intensity of this \nsystem remains uncertain, models are continuing to come into \nbetter agreement, and there is currently a 20-50% chance of more \nthan 0.25\" of rain and SWE over the plains, highest over south \ncentral Montana. During the overnight hours, when lows are in the \n20s to low 30s, some of this precipitation may fall as snow over \nthe plains. Currently, there is a 10-30% chance for at least 0.1\" \nof snow over the plains. Above 7,000 ft, there is a 40-60% chance \nfor at least 4\" of snow. \n\nFor the end of the work week, upper level southwest flow sets up,\nallowing for occasional rounds of precipitation to continue. \nArcher",
